# GETTY(8)

## NAME

getty - set typewriter mode and read a login name

## SYNOPSIS

| **/etc/getty** [ *char* ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Getty* is invoked by init(8) immediately after a typewriter is opened. It reads the user's
login name and calls login(1) with the name as argument.

*Init* calls *getty* with a single character argument taken from the ttys(5) file entry for the
terminal line. That argument selects an entry of a table built into *getty*, giving the modes
the line is set to while the name is read, the modes it is left in for *login*, and the
`login:` greeting message. An argument naming no entry selects the first.

The user's name is terminated by a new-line or carriage-return character. In the second case
CRMOD mode is set (see ioctl(2)).

The name is scanned to see if it contains any lower-case alphabetic characters; if not, and if
the name is nonempty, the system is told to map any future upper-case characters into the
corresponding lower-case characters.

Finally, *login* is called with the user's name as argument.

## THIS MACHINE

**Note:** the table has ONE entry, `0`, and it describes the Consul-254 typewriter, which is
the only kind of terminal this machine has. The line is read RAW and handed to *login* as
ECHO|CRMOD|XTABS, which is what the driver (sc(4), kernel/dev/sc.c) sets on a first open.

v7's table had thirteen entries of PDP-11 baud rates, and none of it survives, because none of
it describes a parallel character-at-a-time typewriter:

speeds
: There is no baud rate. *Sg_ispeed* and *sg_ospeed* are stored by ioctl(2) and read by nothing,
  so v7's `3` entry - which probed the connect speed and chose a table from it - could only ever
  have read back B0. The **break** key, which in v7 made *getty* cycle to the next speed in the
  entry's chain, has nothing to cycle to and no way to be pressed.

delays
: The CR, NL, TAB and FF delay bits are honoured by this kernel: they queue a delay byte that the
  driver turns into a *timeout*(9). They exist for a carriage that needs time to return, which
  this one does not, so no entry here sets them.

parity
: The driver sends and receives seven bits, so ANYP, EVENP and ODDP describe nothing. v7's
  *getty* also ORed an even-parity bit into every character it printed, from a 128-byte table;
  that table is gone.

LCASE
: The Consul's own character code has no lower-case Latin letters, so the line runs in ASCII and
  the driver does not fold case. A *getty* that set LCASE would undo that.

## FILES

/etc/ttys

## SEE ALSO

init(8), login(1), ioctl(2), ttys(5)
